<div class="article-body">

<p>Cucumber leaves turning yellow indoors is normal for the first two or three bottom leaves, and it is a real problem after that. Same color, same place on the plant, opposite fix. One means the vine is retiring an old leaf as it climbs, the other means the pot it lives in has quietly stopped working.</p>

<p>The way you tell them apart is not the color of the leaf. It is the order the yellow moves up the plant, the pattern it makes on the leaf, and one number you can measure in thirty seconds. I got this wrong twice, and the second time cost me a plant that was already holding twelve cucumbers.</p>

<p>Here is the honest part up front: my fall run looked perfect in September. Twelve cucumbers by day 60 from a single vine, which is the best run I have had. Then on day 55 of the next round, six lower leaves went yellow at the edges, so I did what any self-taught grower does when a heavy feeder starts looking tired. I fed it. Nine days later, 14 of my 18 leaves were yellow, the bottom four were dry and crisp, and my meter was reading 3.2 when a fruiting cucumber wants to live between 2.0 and 2.5.</p>

<p>So the yellowing was not the problem. My response to the yellowing was the problem. Here is how to avoid doing that, in the order I now check it.</p>

<h2 id="order">1. The Leaf Order Rule: Which End of the Plant Goes Yellow First</h2>

<p>This is the first thing to look at, and it takes two seconds. Does the yellow start at the bottom and move up, or does it start at the top on the newest growth?</p>

<p>Here is the key insight, and once you know it you can read any plant in the room: nutrients are either mobile or immobile inside the plant. Nitrogen, phosphorus, potassium and magnesium are mobile. When the plant runs short, it pulls those nutrients out of an old leaf and ships them to the growing tip, because the tip is where the future is. That is why old leaves go first. Iron, calcium and boron are immobile, so a shortage shows up on the newest leaves instead, because the plant cannot move the supply up to them.</p>

<p>Cucumbers follow this exactly. The New South Wales Department of Primary Industries guide for greenhouse cucumbers splits its whole diagnosis table on that one line: symptoms in older leaves, or symptoms in younger leaves.</p>

<p>Which means a yellow bottom leaf is the plant telling you it is shipping something out of it. Either it is retiring that leaf on purpose because the vine is now six feet tall and that leaf is in the shade, or one specific nutrient is running low, or the roots have stopped being able to take anything up at all. Three very different stories, and the leaf order only gets you to the right chapter. The pattern gets you to the page.</p>

<p>Get the order wrong and everything after it is guesswork. I spent two weeks staring at my dying plant from above, where all 18 leaves look roughly the same, instead of from the side, where the order of the yellow is obvious.</p>

<h2 id="patterns">2. The Five Yellow Patterns, and What Each One Is Asking For</h2>

<p>Now read the leaf itself. Not the plant, the leaf. The pattern is where the yellow sits on the blade, and each pattern points at a different cause.</p>

<table>
  <thead>
    <tr><th>What you see</th><th>Where it starts</th><th>What it usually is</th></tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>One or two lower leaves pale, rest of the plant healthy, vine still climbing</td><td>Oldest leaves</td><td>Normal ageing. Do nothing.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Even, uniform yellow across the whole blade, plant looks slow and small</td><td>Oldest leaves, moving up</td><td>Nitrogen shortage</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Yellow between the veins while the veins stay green, deep in the leaf</td><td>Oldest leaves</td><td>Magnesium shortage</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Yellow and scorching around the leaf edges, plus between the main veins</td><td>Oldest leaves</td><td>Potassium shortage</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Yellow veins or a green vein skeleton on the newest little leaves at the tip</td><td>Youngest leaves</td><td>Iron shortage, usually a pH problem</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Plant droopy at midday, leaves dull and leathery, thin yellow rim on old leaves</td><td>Oldest leaves</td><td>Too much fertilizer, salty medium</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

<p>Two of these are worth a longer look, because they are the two that actually happened at my house.</p>

<p>Pay attention to this number: magnesium deficiency shows up as the areas between the veins going yellow while the veins themselves stay green. That is the textbook picture, and it is what every article will tell you to look for. Cucumbers are unusually sensitive to it, more than most vegetables, and high potassium makes it worse because potassium and magnesium compete for the same ride into the root. That detail matters later.</p>

<p>The nitrogen one is the other confusing one, because it is not a pattern at all. It is the absence of a pattern. The whole leaf fades evenly, starting at the bottom, and the plant just stops being vigorous. N is easy to fix and the response is fast: a proper nitrogen correction shows on new growth within 3 to 7 days. Magnesium is slower, closer to 2 to 3 weeks. If your fix worked in four days, you guessed right. If nothing changed in a week, you were wrong about which one it was.</p>

<h2 id="edge">3. The Yellow Leaf Edge That Means Two Opposite Things</h2>

<p>I thought I had it figured out. Yellow edge on the old leaves, scorching between the veins, plant still setting fruit. That is potassium shortage, which is the most common disorder in greenhouse cucumbers, and a heavy fruiting vine needs a lot of it. So I switched to a bloom feed and doubled up.</p>

<p>Then it got worse, fast, and I went back and read the rest of that same government guide instead of just the table I wanted.</p>

<p>Here is the part that made me put the bottle down. That same page lists a yellow rim on the older leaves in a second section, under fertilizer excess, not deficiency. The description is almost word for word: slightly droopy plants, dull leathery leaves, and a thin yellow tinge around the margins of the older leaves. Cucumbers are sensitive to excess fertilizer, it says, which raises the salt level in the medium and causes toxicity, usually from nitrogen.</p>

<p>So the same yellow edge on the same old leaf means either feed me potassium, or stop feeding me entirely. There is no way to tell those apart by looking, and I had spent nine days confidently picking the wrong one. My meter said 3.2. The ceiling for a cucumber in fruit is about 2.5. I was not short on anything. I was drowning the roots in salt and calling it care.</p>

<p>The magnesium picture has the same problem. One agriculture source describes cucumber magnesium deficiency as starting at the leaf margin and moving inward. A 2024 study on cucumber leaf magnesium in Agronomy reported the opposite detail, that the margin stayed green while the tissue between the veins stripped out. Both are describing magnesium. The pattern you were told to memorize is not stable enough to bet a plant on.</p>

<p>This is why the diagnosis cannot end at a leaf photo. Position gets you to the chapter, pattern gets you to the page, and one measurement settles the argument. Mine was an EC reading, and it took eleven days and one dead vine to think of taking it.</p>

<h2 id="indoor">4. Why This Goes Wrong Indoors and Not in a Garden Bed</h2>

<p>Every yellow-leaf article on the internet was written for a garden. Rows, rain, soil, worms, drainage, the whole thing. An indoor cucumber in a pot on a shelf has none of that, which changes which cause is most likely.</p>

<p>The first indoor-only cause is pot size. Cucumbers are one plant per pot, and the pot needs to be five gallons of medium. This is not about elbow room, it is about water. A mature cucumber drinks one to two gallons a day when it is fruiting, so a small pot is a pot that swings from soaked to dry twice a day. Waterlogged and bone dry look identical from the top of the plant, and both end in yellowing leaves. A grower on r/Hydroponics posted exactly this with a cucumber in an 11 litre bucket, about 3 gallons, sitting yellow and stunted while the parameters all read fine.</p>

<p>The second is root temperature, and this one is easy to miss because nobody puts a thermometer in the pot. The root zone wants to sit between 65F and 75F. My spare room is 68F, which is fine, but the air right under a grow light at the canopy runs 8 to 12 degrees warmer than the room, and a black pot sitting in that warm zone bakes. Hot roots stop taking up water properly, and the plant wilts at midday even though the medium is wet. If your plant is droopy at 2pm and fine at 8am, stop reading leaves and go check the pot.</p>

<p>The third is the one that got me: no flush. In a garden bed, rain washes excess salt past the root zone. In a pot, whatever you pour in stays there, and the concentration creeps up every week unless you deliberately run water through the medium and let it drain out the bottom. I had not flushed since August. That is how a plant that is being fed more than it needs reads as starving.</p>

<p>If you want to see what that does to the numbers before you spend on anything else, the <a href="/posts/hydroponic-nutrient-guide">hydroponic nutrient guide</a> covers the ranges and how to hold them, and the <a href="/posts/hydroponic-water-temperature">water temperature guide</a> has the root-zone side of the same story. For light height, which decides how much heat those roots are sitting in, use the <a href="/posts/grow-light-distance-chart">grow light distance chart</a>.</p>

<div class="callout">
  <div class="callout-title">The Three-Second Check Before Any Fix</div>
  <p>Look at the leaf order first (bottom up, or top down?). Then the pattern (even yellow, between the veins, or around the edges?). Then measure: pot weight, root zone temperature, and the EC or TDS number if you have a meter. Fix nothing until you have all three. Guessing at this is how you feed a plant that is already overfed.</p>
</div>

<h2 id="burn">5. Nitrogen Hunger vs Nitrogen Burn on a Heavy Feeder</h2>

<p>Cucumbers are heavy feeders. Every guide says it and it is true, which is exactly why this pair catches people. The label makes it worse, because the dose on the bottle assumes a plant in a bed with rain pushing the salt away.</p>

<p>Hunger looks like a plant that fades. The oldest leaves go pale and even, the vine loses its snap, new growth comes in smaller than the last set, and nothing about it is crispy. It looks tired.</p>

<p>Burn looks like a plant that is trying too hard. Leaves are dark and slightly glossy, then dull and leathery, the plant droops a bit through the day even though the medium is damp, and the yellow sits in a thin rim around the edges of the old leaves. Sometimes the tips and edges go brown and brittle. That was mine, and the crisp brown margin on my bottom four leaves was the single clearest symptom I ignored for a week, because I was looking for a deficiency pattern and not considering the plant had too much.</p>

<p>Here is why that matters: the two fixes are exact opposites. Hunger needs a feeding. Burn needs a flush, plain water through the medium until it runs out the bottom, and then nothing but water for a week.</p>

<p>The good news is that cucumber is a fast plant and it tells you which one you picked. Feed a hungry plant and the new growth is visibly darker within 3 to 7 days. Flush a salty plant and the older leaves stop getting worse within about 4 days, though they never go green again. That last part is worth stating plainly, because it is the question everyone asks.</p>

<h2 id="cut">6. Should You Cut the Yellow Leaves Off?</h2>

<p>No, a yellow cucumber leaf does not turn green again. Chlorophyll does not come back once the plant has pulled it apart for parts. So the leaf is not going to recover, and the only question left is whether it should stay on the plant.</p>

<p>Three rules I use now.</p>

<p>Cut a leaf if it is more than half yellow and sitting low in the canopy, especially if it is touching the medium or lying against another leaf. Those are the leaves that rot and invite problems.</p>

<p>Leave a leaf if it is less than half yellow and still in the light. Even a tired leaf is still making food, and on a heavy fruiting vine you need every one you have.</p>

<p>Never strip the bottom clean just because it looks untidy. I did that once in a panic and the plant immediately redirected energy into four new side shoots instead of fruit. A cucumber with nothing to photosynthesize with will build leaves before it builds cucumbers, every time.</p>

<p>The real reason to open up the lower canopy is airflow, not tidiness. Powdery mildew on an indoor cucumber starts on the crowded lower leaves, where the air does not move. Take out the dead ones, take out anything touching a neighbour, and leave a leaf's worth of space between the rest.</p>

<h2 id="fix">7. The 3 Ways to Fix This, and the $34 One I Run</h2>

<p>There are three ways this goes, and I have now done the first one twice.</p>

<p><strong>The trap.</strong> Read the leaf, decide it is a deficiency, feed harder. It costs nothing to try, which is why everyone does it, and on a pot that has been fed all season it usually turns a slow yellow into a fast one. That was my fall run: twelve cucumbers in hand, nine days of extra feed, and a plant with four crisp leaves left.</p>

<p><strong>The overkill.</strong> A Dutch bucket, a recirculating reservoir, dosing pumps, a daily pH and EC log. Commercial greenhouse cucumber growers do all of that and none of it is wrong. You have one vine on a shelf in a spare room, and a reservoir you have to clean is a reservoir you will stop cleaning by November.</p>

<p><strong>The middle ground I actually run.</strong> Three things, about $34, and the first one is the only one that matters.</p>

<ul>
  <li>An <a href="https://www.amazon.com/s?k=EC+TDS+meter+hydroponics&tag=tabletopharve-20" target="_blank" rel="nofollow sponsored">EC TDS meter</a>, about $16. This ends the argument between hunger and burn in thirty seconds. Mine read 3.2 when the plant wanted 2.0 to 2.5. Nothing I had read on a leaf was ever going to tell me that.</li>
  <li>A <a href="https://www.amazon.com/s?k=5+gallon+fabric+pot&tag=tabletopharve-20" target="_blank" rel="nofollow sponsored">5 gallon fabric pot</a>, about $12 for a pack. The fabric sides let the medium breathe and dry evenly, and a pot I can actually carry to the sink is a pot I will actually flush. The <a href="/posts/how-to-grow-cucumbers-indoors">indoor cucumber growing guide</a> covers why it is one plant per pot and not two.</li>
  <li><a href="https://www.amazon.com/s?k=magnesium+sulfate+epsom+salt&tag=tabletopharve-20" target="_blank" rel="nofollow sponsored">Magnesium sulfate (Epsom salt)</a>, about $8 for a bag that will outlive the plant. One tablespoon per gallon as a leaf spray, once, after lights off, only if the pattern is yellow between green veins. Garden extension guidance goes as high as two tablespoons per gallon, and magnesium does not move into leaves easily, so a spray beats a pour for a fast answer.</li>
</ul>

<p>Here is the honest truth. That leaf spray works, and that is exactly what makes it a trap. My sprayed leaves were visibly deeper green in five days, which felt like a cure and was not one, because the margin yellowing kept marching up the vine the whole time. A green leaf spray tells you the plant can still take up magnesium. It tells you nothing about why it was struggling in the first place, and if the answer is salt, you have made the pot saltier. Spray the leaves, but flush the pot in the same week or you have just painted over the problem.</p>

<p><strong>Bottom line:</strong> check the order, check the pattern, then measure before you pour anything. If you are buying one thing, buy the <a href="https://www.amazon.com/s?k=EC+TDS+meter+hydroponics&tag=tabletopharve-20" target="_blank" rel="nofollow sponsored">EC TDS meter</a>. It was about $16 when I last looked and these move between $12 and $24 depending on whether you get a combo pH pen, so anything under $18 is a solid deal. Then a <a href="https://www.amazon.com/s?k=5+gallon+fabric+pot&tag=tabletopharve-20" target="_blank" rel="nofollow sponsored">5 gallon fabric pot</a> so the roots get air and you can flush without a fight, and Epsom salt only after the pattern says magnesium and the meter says the salt level is sane.</p>

<p>The yellow leaf is not the disease. It is the receipt. And in a pot, the receipt is almost never asking for more fertilizer. Let the $34 of measuring tools cost you less than my $38 of bloom feed did.</p>

<p>One thing worth noting if you are on a hydro system rather than a pot: the general <a href="/posts/hydroponic-leaves-turning-yellow">hydroponic leaves turning yellow</a> guide covers the reservoir version of this, where the cause is more often pH lockout than pot size, and this article is the cucurbit-specific branch of it. If your vine is also stalling instead of just yellowing, the vine is usually the same story. If you are shopping for a light that can carry a fruiting vine without cooking the pot underneath it, compare the measured output numbers on the <a href="https://tabletopharvest.com/grow-lights" target="_blank">Grow Light DB</a> rather than trusting the wattage on the box.</p>

